const faqs = [
  ["What is ADHD?", "Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is a neurodevelopmental condition that affects attention, impulse control, and activity level. It’s not a matter of laziness or willpower — it reflects real differences in how the brain manages focus, motivation, and self-regulation. ADHD often appears in childhood, but many people aren’t diagnosed until their teens or adulthood, when the demands of school, work, or relationships make the challenges harder to ignore."],
  ["What are the signs of ADHD?", "ADHD presents in three ways — mainly inattentive, mainly hyperactive-impulsive, or a combination. Common signs include trouble focusing or staying on task; being easily distracted or forgetful; difficulty organizing tasks, time, or belongings; restlessness or fidgeting; impulsive words or actions; starting tasks but struggling to finish; and frequently losing things or running late. In adults, hyperactivity often looks more like inner restlessness and difficulty with follow-through."],
  ["How is ADHD diagnosed?", "ADHD is diagnosed through a careful clinical evaluation rather than a single test. At Hopewell Health Solutions, that may include a structured ADHD evaluation and, when a fuller picture is helpful, neuropsychological testing to assess attention, memory, and learning and to rule out other conditions. A thorough assessment ensures the diagnosis is accurate and that treatment — and any school or work accommodations — is built on solid ground."],
  ["How is ADHD treated?", "ADHD is highly manageable. Treatment at Hopewell Health Solutions is tailored to the individual and often combines medication management with skills-based and behavioral support for focus, organization, and emotional regulation. For children and teens, we involve the whole family and support executive-function development. Because ADHD frequently co-occurs with anxiety, depression, or learning differences, we treat the whole picture rather than the label alone."],
  ["Can adults have ADHD?", "Yes. Many adults have ADHD that went unrecognized in childhood — often because they learned to compensate or mask it. In adulthood, ADHD can show up as chronic disorganization, missed deadlines, restlessness, difficulty finishing tasks, or struggles with relationships and self-esteem. An evaluation can bring clarity, and treatment can make a real difference at any age."],
